@page "/docs/get-started/existing-app"

@* izzy init (catalog/init, Setup.cs) and what's already in an app. Each kind of app was checked against
   Microsoft's .NET 10 templates (empty and full Blazor Web App, Auto, WebAssembly, MVC, Razor Pages): it builds
   after init. The Bootstrap layering was checked in Chromium against Bootstrap 5.3 and the template's app.css;
   the EditForm case against a .NET 10 app. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="existing-app">
    <Lead>
        <Code>izzy init</Code> sets up IzzyBlazor in an existing Blazor Web App, MVC app or Razor Pages app. Your pages,
        forms and components keep working, and you can move to IzzyBlazor one page at a time.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Set it up</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Install the CLI, then run <Code>izzy init</Code> in the project's folder. For an app with a <Code>.Client</Code>
            project, run it in the solution's folder:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">@Init</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            Init adds the package and the changes below, then builds the app. To see the changes before init writes anything,
            run <Code>izzy init --dry-run --diff</Code>. Running init a second time changes nothing.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Changes" Caption="What izzy init changes" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="App" Width="Length.Rem(11)">@context!.App</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What izzy init does">@context!.Change</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            Init doesn't set up a standalone Blazor WebAssembly app, a Blazor Server app with a <Code>_Host.cshtml</Code>,
            or an app on a .NET version below 10. In those apps it stops and explains why. Init accepts these options:
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Options" Caption="izzy init options" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(15)"><code>@context!.App</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Change</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            To make each change yourself instead, see <Anchor Href="docs/get-started/by-hand">Set up by hand</Anchor>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">MVC and Razor Pages</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            Init adds a Blazor root beside your views: <Code>Components/App.razor</Code>, a router and a layout. New pages
            go in <Code>Components/Pages</Code> as Razor components with <Code>@@page</Code>, and use that layout. Your views
            and Razor Pages keep <Code>_Layout.cshtml</Code> and Bootstrap, so IzzyBlazor's CSS and Bootstrap never style
            the same page.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            When a component and a Razor Page have the same URL, the component handles it and the Razor Page no longer
            shows. <Code>izzy add legal</Code> warns when <Code>Pages/Privacy.cshtml</Code> exists. To give the new page
            another URL, use <Code>--route</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Keep Bootstrap</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's CSS is in cascade layers, so any of your CSS outside a layer wins over it. That makes IzzyBlazor
            easy to adjust. It also means Bootstrap, and rules such as the template's <Code>a { color: … }</Code>, override
            IzzyBlazor's components. Button links turn blue on blue, and headings change size.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            When <Code>App.razor</Code> links Bootstrap, init moves Bootstrap into its own layer, declared before
            IzzyBlazor's. Init removes the <Code>&lt;link&gt;</Code>, imports Bootstrap at the top of <Code>app.css</Code>,
            and links <Code>app.css</Code> before IzzyBlazor's stylesheet. Your stylesheet and <Code>App.razor</Code> then
            look like this:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="css" Title="wwwroot/app.css">@AppCss</Code>
        <Code Block Language="razor" Title="Components/App.razor" HighlightLines="1-2">@AppHead</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A layer declared first loses to the layers after it, so IzzyBlazor's components win over Bootstrap. Bootstrap's
            classes still style your existing markup.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Your rules outside a layer still win over both. Init lists the rules in <Code>app.css</Code> that style bare
            elements, such as <Code>a</Code> or <Code>html, body</Code>. To keep them for your pages, move them into
            <Code>@@layer bootstrap { }</Code>, as above. To leave Bootstrap where it was, use <Code>--keep-css</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Alert Status="Status.Info" Title="Your pages get IzzyBlazor's page defaults">
            IzzyBlazor sets the page's font, text color and background, and its focus ring. Once Bootstrap is in a layer, those
            apply to your existing pages too. To keep your own, set them outside a layer, in <Code>app.css</Code>.
        </Alert>

        <Heading Level="2">Name clashes</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's components have plain names, such as <Code>Button</Code>, <Code>Card</Code> and <Code>NavMenu</Code>.
            When a component of yours has the same name, the build fails with
            <Code>RZ9985: Multiple components use the tag 'NavMenu'</Code>. Init fixes this by writing your component's full
            name wherever you use it, as with the full template's own <Code>NavMenu</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">&lt;MyApp.Components.Layout.NavMenu /&gt;</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A type with the same name, such as your own <Code>Status</Code> enum, causes an ambiguous reference error
            instead (<Code>CS0104</Code>). Init can't find these until the app builds, so it reports them after its build.
            To fix one, qualify the name (<Code>IzzyBlazor.Status.Success</Code>) or add an alias to
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@@using Status = IzzyBlazor.Status</Code>

        <Heading Level="2">One folder at a time</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code> applies to its folder and the folders under it. To move to IzzyBlazor gradually, move
            <Code>@@using IzzyBlazor</Code> from <Code>Components/_Imports.razor</Code> to a folder's own
            <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>. Only the pages in that folder see IzzyBlazor's components.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>App.razor</Code> and any layout built with IzzyBlazor need the <Code>@@using</Code> too, at the top of the
            file. Without it, Razor treats <Code>&lt;IzzyTheme /&gt;</Code> as an unknown HTML element. The build only
            warns, and the theme never renders.
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="text">@Folders</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            A page can use a new layout while the other pages keep yours. Build the layout with
            <Anchor Href="docs/components/app-shell">AppShell</Anchor>. Set it on the page with <Code>@@layout</Code>, or make
            it a folder's default layout in that folder's <Code>_Imports.razor</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Forms you already have</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's fields are Blazor input components. They work inside your existing <Code>EditForm</Code>, beside
            Blazor's own inputs, with the validator you already use:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="razor">@EditFormCode</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            IzzyBlazor's fields display their own label, hint and errors. To get IzzyBlazor's form layout and columns,
            replace the <Code>EditForm</Code> with IzzyBlazor's <Anchor Href="docs/components/form">Form</Anchor>. Form has
            the same <Code>Model</Code>, <Code>FormName</Code> and submit events, and adds the DataAnnotations validator
            for you.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/cli/overview">The izzy CLI</Anchor>: add blocks and pages to the app as code you own.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components">Components</Anchor>: every component, with what it does in each render mode.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/components/izzy-theme">IzzyTheme</Anchor>: match your brand color and fonts.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string App, string Change);

    private const string Init = """
        dotnet tool install -g IzzyBlazor.Cli --prerelease
        cd MyApp
        izzy init
        """;

    private static readonly Row[] Changes =
    [
        new("Blazor Web App", "Adds the IzzyBlazor package, the stylesheet and IzzyTheme in App.razor, @using IzzyBlazor in _Imports.razor, AddIzzyBlazor() in Program.cs, and a ToastRegion in the layout the router uses."),
        new("With .Client", "Makes the same changes in the server project. Adds IzzyBlazor.Core, @using IzzyBlazor and AddIzzyBlazorCore() to the .Client project."),
        new("MVC, Razor Pages", "Adds the IzzyBlazor package and a Blazor root under Components/ (App, Routes, _Imports and a layout). Adds AddRazorComponents(), UseAntiforgery() and MapRazorComponents<App>() to Program.cs."),
    ];

    private static readonly Row[] Options =
    [
        new("--preset, --brand", "Sets the theme preset (Standard, Compact, Soft, Editorial) and the brand color, as in the starter."),
        new("--interactivity Server", "For MVC and Razor Pages apps, lets a page run on the server over a circuit. Without it, every new page is static."),
        new("--keep-css", "Leaves Bootstrap where App.razor links it."),
        new("--mcp", "Connects AI assistants to IzzyBlazor's docs server. If you already have an MCP config, init leaves it as it is."),
        new("--no-build", "Skips the build at the end."),
    ];

    private const string AppCss = """
        /* First: puts Bootstrap in a layer that IzzyBlazor's layers come after. */
        @import url("lib/bootstrap/dist/css/bootstrap.min.css") layer(bootstrap);

        /* Rules for bare elements, in the same layer, so they don't reach IzzyBlazor's components. */
        @layer bootstrap {
            a, .btn-link { color: #006bb7; }
        }

        /* Everything else as before: outside a layer, these still win. */
        .content { padding-top: 1.1rem; }
        """;

    private const string AppHead = """
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/IzzyBlazor.Core/izzyblazor.css"]" />
        <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["MyApp.styles.css"]" />
        <IzzyTheme />
        """;

    private const string Folders = """
        Components/
          _Imports.razor           your usings, as before
          App.razor                @using IzzyBlazor at the top, for IzzyTheme
          Pages/
            Orders.razor           untouched
            Settings/
              _Imports.razor       @using IzzyBlazor
              Profile.razor        uses IzzyBlazor
        """;

    private const string EditFormCode = """
        <EditForm Model="Model" FormName="profile" OnValidSubmit="Save">
            <DataAnnotationsValidator />
            <TextField @bind-Value="Model!.Email" />
            <InputText @bind-Value="Model!.Nickname" class="form-control" />
            <button type="submit" class="btn btn-primary">Save</button>
        </EditForm>
        """;
}
